00:39:02.070 --> 00:39:07.070
and thereby live a more robust experience.

00:39:07.800 --> 00:39:09.480
If you just think,

00:39:09.480 --> 00:39:12.990
well, I can just feel anxious

00:39:12.990 --> 00:39:16.800
or I just feel sometimes I realize that

00:39:16.800 --> 00:39:19.440
anxiety is actually anger,

00:39:19.440 --> 00:39:22.050
or if I say I'm frustrated, it's like,

00:39:22.050 --> 00:39:23.664
well, that actually may be anger.

00:39:23.664 --> 00:39:26.490
But and then also saying,

00:39:26.490 --> 00:39:28.487
hey, there's lots of different types of,

00:39:28.487 --> 00:39:30.418
there's joy you can experience.

00:39:30.418 --> 00:39:32.520
There's lots of positive emotions too.

00:39:32.520 --> 00:39:34.170
That's what I'm trying to say.

00:39:34.170 --> 00:39:36.693
I wanna experience all of the emotions,

00:39:38.880 --> 00:39:40.320
so that's my recommendation.

00:39:40.320 --> 00:39:41.850
I don't know if it's my rec today,

00:39:41.850 --> 00:39:43.557
but "Atlas of the Heart".

00:39:44.910 --> 00:39:45.840
Yeah, that should be the rec,

00:39:45.840 --> 00:39:47.700
because I don't have one.

00:39:47.700 --> 00:39:48.950
I don't know.

00:39:54.270 --> 00:39:57.020
I don't wanna just go to anxiety.

00:39:59.580 --> 00:40:01.080
That's definitely me.

00:40:01.080 --> 00:40:02.880
Well, just be honest man.

00:40:02.880 --> 00:40:07.880
But I think that that's probably my go-to.

00:40:09.450 --> 00:40:10.350
Yeah, pretty good.

00:40:11.400 --> 00:40:15.120
I got a pretty good
anxious sweet spot there.

00:40:15.120 --> 00:40:16.950
It's just like, nestle
right down in there.

00:40:16.950 --> 00:40:20.040
It's like, let that divot in
your bed where you're like,

00:40:20.040 --> 00:40:22.560
oh, this is where I
lay every single night.

00:40:22.560 --> 00:40:26.260
And I can tell if I shift over
two inches in the wrong way.

00:40:26.260 --> 00:40:31.080
And it's like, oh, I'm back
in that anxious spot again.

00:40:31.080 --> 00:40:32.583
Maybe I should roll over.

00:40:33.960 --> 00:40:35.223
Roll over into joy.

00:40:36.097 --> 00:40:38.403
"Roll Over to Joy" that's your book Link.

00:40:40.530 --> 00:40:45.530
Okay, "Roll Over Into Joy"?

00:40:45.681 --> 00:40:47.880
"Roll Over Into Joy", yeah.

00:40:47.880 --> 00:40:50.853
Okay, I'd read that, I'd
read the first two chapters.

00:40:54.540 --> 00:40:56.163
Is goosebumps an emotion?

00:40:57.180 --> 00:40:58.860
Yeah, potentially.

00:40:58.860 --> 00:41:01.650
But I totally appreciate
what you're saying

00:41:01.650 --> 00:41:03.510
and that's something that
therapy has helped me with

00:41:03.510 --> 00:41:08.510
is you feel something

00:41:09.480 --> 00:41:13.170
and then you are if you lean into it

00:41:13.170 --> 00:41:14.003
and you get to the bottom of it,

00:41:14.003 --> 00:41:14.940
What is that?

00:41:14.940 --> 00:41:16.500
Oh, there's something beneath it.

00:41:16.500 --> 00:41:18.270
But generally speaking,

00:41:18.270 --> 00:41:20.010
and this may be, this is I kind of figured

00:41:20.010 --> 00:41:21.450
that's what your answer would be

00:41:21.450 --> 00:41:24.060
and it may be surprising, it
may be even surprising to you,

00:41:24.060 --> 00:41:28.740
but I think that as you
look at a list of emotions,

00:41:28.740 --> 00:41:32.610
I think that anxiety is probably
the thing that is operating

00:41:32.610 --> 00:41:36.210
the most often for me as well.

00:41:36.210 --> 00:41:39.900
Now I may deal with it or it
may manifest itself differently

00:41:39.900 --> 00:41:41.250
and people may not be like,

00:41:42.900 --> 00:41:44.550
oh, I wouldn't have, you may not know that

00:41:44.550 --> 00:41:45.900
even if you're interacting with me,

00:41:45.900 --> 00:41:49.170
I don't tend to let you know
that that's what's happening.

00:41:49.170 --> 00:41:50.880
But I was thinking about
the way that I interact with

00:41:50.880 --> 00:41:52.470
this this weekend.

00:41:52.470 --> 00:41:57.470
Yeah, one huge part of it is anticipation

00:41:58.860 --> 00:42:01.980
and just knowing that we've thought about

00:42:01.980 --> 00:42:04.320
creating this experience for you guys

00:42:04.320 --> 00:42:09.090
that we've got a team
that knows you really well

00:42:09.090 --> 00:42:11.100
and knows what you like and what you want

00:42:11.100 --> 00:42:13.230
and picking a special spot like this.

00:42:13.230 --> 00:42:15.540
But then right on the other side of

00:42:15.540 --> 00:42:18.450
every one of those anticipations

00:42:18.450 --> 00:42:22.020
is an anxiety about something
that could go wrong,

00:42:22.020 --> 00:42:23.400
is the fear is we're gonna come down

00:42:23.400 --> 00:42:25.600
and just roll through the whole town

00:42:26.970 --> 00:42:30.123
just taking people one
by one and throwing them.

00:42:34.230 --> 00:42:35.107
And then you're like,

00:42:35.107 --> 00:42:38.220
"Oh, we made a decision
to do this outdoor event,

00:42:38.220 --> 00:42:39.510
was that a mistake?

00:42:39.510 --> 00:42:40.343
Is it gonna rain?

00:42:40.343 --> 00:42:41.997
Yes, it's gonna rain on day 1."

00:42:45.210 --> 00:42:49.050
And then things like, okay,
oh, the performance last...

00:42:49.050 --> 00:42:52.590
I'm like choosing, where
am I gonna place my worry?

00:42:52.590 --> 00:42:55.740
It's like, okay, I'm
gonna do this concert,

00:42:55.740 --> 00:42:58.470
perform these songs that I've
never performed for a crowd.

00:42:58.470 --> 00:43:00.900
You really wanna kind of
have warmed up a little bit

00:43:00.900 --> 00:43:02.250
in front of some people,

00:43:02.250 --> 00:43:03.540
but, oh, I don't have time to do that,

00:43:03.540 --> 00:43:06.180
so let's, oh, it'll be
fine, let's just do it.

00:43:06.180 --> 00:43:08.700
Jesse's got anxiety about it

00:43:08.700 --> 00:43:10.860
and then there's there
just all the things.

00:43:10.860 --> 00:43:12.990
And then we've got this ridiculous show

00:43:12.990 --> 00:43:14.190
that we're gonna do tonight

00:43:14.190 --> 00:43:17.970
where we're only doing the
things that we're gonna do,

00:43:17.970 --> 00:43:19.650
because it's you guys and we know

00:43:19.650 --> 00:43:21.990
that you'll have a fun time
watching us do something

00:43:21.990 --> 00:43:24.901
that we probably wouldn't
take out on the road.

00:43:28.050 --> 00:43:31.710
But yeah, so it's like a lot of,

00:43:31.710 --> 00:43:33.840
and I don't necessarily
think I would call it

00:43:35.130 --> 00:43:36.480
an anxiety disorder,

00:43:36.480 --> 00:43:41.370
but it is definitely the general
thing that's always there,

00:43:41.370 --> 00:43:44.610
just under the surface that
you can dip into at any point.

00:43:44.610 --> 00:43:45.813
To be like, "Okay, yep,

00:43:45.813 --> 00:43:48.420
that's something I can attach
some energy to right now

00:43:48.420 --> 00:43:49.830
and worry and try to figure out."

00:43:49.830 --> 00:43:52.380
Okay, I'm gonna change my answer to horny.

00:43:52.380 --> 00:43:57.150
Okay, I don't believe that's an emotion.

00:43:57.150 --> 00:43:59.943
You might wanna talk to
your therapist about that.

00:44:02.370 --> 00:44:04.410
That's like saying that
your love language is sex,

00:44:04.410 --> 00:44:06.169
which I tried to pull that one time.

00:44:09.600 --> 00:44:10.740
Oh really, it's not.

00:44:10.740 --> 00:44:12.780
Yeah, it can't be, it's not an emotion.

00:44:12.780 --> 00:44:13.920
It could be physical touch,

00:44:13.920 --> 00:44:16.653
but not always that
kind of physical touch.

00:44:18.510 --> 00:44:21.030
Okay, but then I'll
just go with goosebumps.

00:44:21.030 --> 00:44:21.863
Okay.

00:44:23.513 --> 00:44:27.383
I feel like I've been pretty chill here.

00:44:27.383 --> 00:44:30.060
No, and I think Jesse
and I were talking about

00:44:30.060 --> 00:44:31.110
it last night.

00:44:31.110 --> 00:44:32.010
How chill I am?

00:44:32.010 --> 00:44:33.310
Yeah, how chill Link is,

00:44:35.940 --> 00:44:38.026
because the thing that
you wanna be able to do is

00:44:38.026 --> 00:44:41.220
and I feel like with each
year that I get older,

00:44:41.220 --> 00:44:45.750
I get better at this, is
not just wanting something

00:44:45.750 --> 00:44:46.830
to be over.

00:44:46.830 --> 00:44:49.080
There's so much of my life
when I've had something like

00:44:49.080 --> 00:44:50.250
that performance last night

00:44:50.250 --> 00:44:51.390
or what we're doing tonight

00:44:51.390 --> 00:44:53.910
where I'm just, I'm actually
sort of fantasizing about

00:44:53.910 --> 00:44:55.983
getting to my hotel room and lying down.

00:44:58.023 --> 00:45:00.810
And they probably don't want to hear that.

00:45:00.810 --> 00:45:02.790
Yeah, right, 'cause they're
just, "Get through it,

00:45:02.790 --> 00:45:05.460
don't screw it up and just
get to your hotel bed."

00:45:05.460 --> 00:45:08.190
But that you're acknowledging

00:45:08.190 --> 00:45:10.207
that something's wrong with that.

00:45:10.207 --> 00:45:15.207
Yeah, and actually well, and
being able to talk to Jesse

00:45:15.930 --> 00:45:18.070
throughout the past months

00:45:19.050 --> 00:45:22.500
about her being a part of
this actually was helpful.

00:45:22.500 --> 00:45:24.000
Being able to tell her
some of those things

00:45:24.000 --> 00:45:26.280
that I would tell myself
was therapeutic for me,

00:45:26.280 --> 00:45:30.210
'cause I was like, "Listen,
the experience is going to be

00:45:30.210 --> 00:45:35.210
when it's happening,
don't let that slip by.

00:45:35.640 --> 00:45:37.320
Anticipate it, look forward to it,

00:45:37.320 --> 00:45:40.170
and then enjoy it when it's happening."

00:45:40.170 --> 00:45:42.720
And I think that by
talking about that so much,

00:45:42.720 --> 00:45:44.940
I actually, when it was over,

00:45:44.940 --> 00:45:46.140
I didn't have a sense of relief,

00:45:46.140 --> 00:45:50.070
I was like, "Oh man, I'd
like to do that again,"

00:45:50.070 --> 00:45:52.230
or, "I wish that had gone on longer.

00:45:52.230 --> 00:45:53.710
I wish that was still happening."

00:45:56.190 --> 00:45:57.060
That's good.

00:45:57.060 --> 00:45:59.970
Yeah, so I'm getting better at that.

00:45:59.970 --> 00:46:03.030
So you didn't run to your bed afterward?

00:46:03.030 --> 00:46:04.530
Well, we did run to the bed.

00:46:06.330 --> 00:46:10.620
So, what are we learning here?

00:46:10.620 --> 00:46:15.363
Yeah, it's like we've had the privilege

00:46:16.350 --> 00:46:19.683
of experiencing so many things.

00:46:21.474 --> 00:46:26.474
We lead a really wild life being able to

00:46:27.330 --> 00:46:29.763
just live our dreams,

00:46:29.763 --> 00:46:33.600
but yeah, there's something
to like we wrote about

00:46:33.600 --> 00:46:37.950
in the book, like making a
decision to stop and celebrate,

00:46:37.950 --> 00:46:38.783
you know what I'm saying?

00:46:38.783 --> 00:46:43.017
Like, "This moment, I'm
here for it, I'm in it."

00:46:44.370 --> 00:46:49.370
And I just wanna go back
to giving you guys credit

00:46:49.740 --> 00:46:54.510
that you've created an environment

00:46:54.510 --> 00:46:58.980
for us here that I'm not freaking out,

00:46:58.980 --> 00:47:03.980
but instead I'm like, they
know what they're here for

00:47:04.080 --> 00:47:05.430
and they know what they're gonna get.

00:47:05.430 --> 00:47:09.033
So I just, let's just go with it.

00:47:10.129 --> 00:47:12.350
You might as well not try too hard,

00:47:12.350 --> 00:47:16.650
just let it happen, let it happen,

00:47:16.650 --> 00:47:18.330
it's a safe space.

00:47:18.330 --> 00:47:23.330
So I do feel like

00:47:23.550 --> 00:47:24.997
I'm living it up.

00:47:28.133 --> 00:47:29.550
I feel like I'm here-
I'm having a good time.

00:47:29.550 --> 00:47:30.383
I'm having a good time

00:47:30.383 --> 00:47:35.360
and it's so much different
when you go into a place

00:47:36.270 --> 00:47:40.800
where you're having to explain yourself

00:47:40.800 --> 00:47:42.480
or prove yourself.

00:47:42.480 --> 00:47:44.550
So that's what I was talking to Jesse,

00:47:44.550 --> 00:47:48.160
I was like, this weekend is built on

00:47:49.830 --> 00:47:52.470
for some of you, it may be
days or weeks or months,

00:47:52.470 --> 00:47:56.463
but many of you years of a relationship,

00:47:57.360 --> 00:47:59.880
that we get to come in here
and have together for a weekend

00:47:59.880 --> 00:48:01.593
and experience in person.

00:48:05.490 --> 00:48:07.230
Jamie, how are we doing on time?

00:48:07.230 --> 00:48:08.310
We got-

00:48:08.310 --> 00:48:09.910
When are we supposed to be done?

00:48:10.800 --> 00:48:12.930
I think we're just rolling
right along, we're good.

00:48:12.930 --> 00:48:13.763
Oh, we're good.

00:48:13.763 --> 00:48:18.710
So Mike Davis, right there
in the leopard print.

00:48:22.890 --> 00:48:23.970
How you're doing?
Hey, Mike.

00:48:23.970 --> 00:48:25.980
Hey.
What up Mike Davis?

00:48:25.980 --> 00:48:27.000
What's going on?

00:48:27.000 --> 00:48:29.107
I don't think we've
ever answered this one.

00:48:29.107 --> 00:48:31.020
"What's your least favorite episode

00:48:31.020 --> 00:48:32.320
of Good Mythical Morning?"

00:48:36.480 --> 00:48:37.770
Least favorite episode?

00:48:37.770 --> 00:48:39.360
Do you have an answer?

00:48:39.360 --> 00:48:40.907
Somebody yelled out, "Ghost Pepper."

00:48:40.907 --> 00:48:43.830
I mean, the Carolina Reaper.

00:48:43.830 --> 00:48:45.030
Wasn't it worse?

00:48:45.030 --> 00:48:45.873
It was worse.

00:48:47.831 --> 00:48:50.182
Blind Taco, The Horseshoe.

00:48:50.182 --> 00:48:51.747
Well, the ones that definitely-

00:48:51.747 --> 00:48:53.460
People think The Horseshoe,

00:48:53.460 --> 00:48:55.770
people think the Blind Taco, blind?

00:48:55.770 --> 00:48:56.940
Blood Taco.

00:48:56.940 --> 00:48:58.320
I was like, what's a Blind Taco?

00:48:58.320 --> 00:49:01.800
I mean, if I think about
the physical effects

00:49:01.800 --> 00:49:05.790
of an episode, I don't
even remember what it was.

00:49:05.790 --> 00:49:07.710
It wasn't at The Ghost
Pepper, it was that other one,

00:49:07.710 --> 00:49:10.530
The Scorpion Pepper where I
was in good Mythical more,

00:49:10.530 --> 00:49:12.000
I was lying on the couch.

00:49:12.000 --> 00:49:13.020
Oh yeah.

00:49:13.020 --> 00:49:16.650
And I was just, I had lost the
capacity for entertainment.

00:49:16.650 --> 00:49:19.800
I was beyond being energized
by the what was happening.

00:49:19.800 --> 00:49:24.790
And I was just angry
and scared and anxious

00:49:26.130 --> 00:49:27.990
and that was bad.

00:49:27.990 --> 00:49:29.970
And then of course, the longest-lasting

00:49:29.970 --> 00:49:34.960
negative physical effects
was that glass of vinegar

00:49:37.050 --> 00:49:38.430
where it just led to like a-

00:49:38.430 --> 00:49:40.140
A duo or don't-o.

00:49:40.140 --> 00:49:41.850
With some mash.

00:49:41.850 --> 00:49:42.683
And it was-

00:49:42.683 --> 00:49:43.516
That was horrible.

00:49:44.640 --> 00:49:45.960
That's the reason we ended up buying them,

00:49:45.960 --> 00:49:47.660
because I was so angry about that.

00:49:52.635 --> 00:49:55.766
It's like, yeah, "Don't
cross me, I'll buy ya."

00:49:55.766 --> 00:50:00.300
To me, you think it's the Grape Nuts vlog.

00:50:00.300 --> 00:50:01.773
Yeah, that was torturous.

00:50:04.680 --> 00:50:06.600
See, I don't have an answer.

00:50:06.600 --> 00:50:08.444
My least favorite GMM episodes

00:50:08.444 --> 00:50:10.263
are the ones where it's like,

00:50:11.430 --> 00:50:14.073
I think it would be if I
could come up with one,

00:50:15.090 --> 00:50:18.840
one that I was really excited
about that then sucked,

00:50:18.840 --> 00:50:20.910
'cause it's like, yeah, the food stuff

00:50:20.910 --> 00:50:22.590
were miserable afterward,

00:50:22.590 --> 00:50:26.820
but it's really hard
to point to an episode

00:50:26.820 --> 00:50:29.160
that was a complete flop.

00:50:29.160 --> 00:50:31.140
I mean, you might not
like some of the guests

00:50:31.140 --> 00:50:33.840
and I'm not gonna throw
any guests under the bus,

00:50:33.840 --> 00:50:35.463
so let's remove those.

00:50:36.376 --> 00:50:39.810
We've had a few guests
that absolutely sucked.

00:50:39.810 --> 00:50:40.800
Yeah.

00:50:40.800 --> 00:50:43.530
Right, I think I might
thought start a Reddit thread

00:50:43.530 --> 00:50:45.060
where everybody can just talk about

00:50:45.060 --> 00:50:46.740
which guests sucked the most.

00:50:46.740 --> 00:50:49.233
That'd be great.
We'll leave that for there.

00:50:52.170 --> 00:50:57.170
But it's a real credit
to the Mythical crew

00:50:57.390 --> 00:50:58.290
that makes the show.

00:50:58.290 --> 00:51:00.420
I mean, you've seen the
evolution of the show.

00:51:00.420 --> 00:51:03.280
It's changing over time

00:51:04.410 --> 00:51:09.410
that they know the show and us so well

00:51:09.480 --> 00:51:14.480
that they're setting up
parameters for the show

00:51:14.580 --> 00:51:17.550
to happen to us.

00:51:17.550 --> 00:51:19.050
It's a really interesting thing

00:51:19.050 --> 00:51:23.400
that the thing our baby

00:51:23.400 --> 00:51:25.897
is now something that
we've basically said,

00:51:25.897 --> 00:51:30.897
"All right, we're entrusting
you to raise our baby team

00:51:31.260 --> 00:51:33.908
and then bring our baby to us and then

00:51:33.908 --> 00:51:36.810
see how we react to it.

00:51:36.810 --> 00:51:38.970
Oh, it's my baby's back.

00:51:38.970 --> 00:51:40.830
Oh, you've gotten taller."

00:51:40.830 --> 00:51:42.510
It's like sending it
off the boarding school.

00:51:42.510 --> 00:51:44.208
So it's this weird, it's this weird

00:51:44.208 --> 00:51:48.690
like being just as committed

00:51:48.690 --> 00:51:50.280
to Good Mythical Morning as ever,

00:51:50.280 --> 00:51:52.860
just as excited about it as ever

00:51:52.860 --> 00:51:55.830
and knowing that there's ways

00:51:55.830 --> 00:51:59.550
that we can infuse ourselves into the show

00:51:59.550 --> 00:52:03.870
even more by entrusting
our team and stepping back

00:52:03.870 --> 00:52:08.580
from the matriculations of planning.

00:52:08.580 --> 00:52:11.430
We've done that
increasingly year over year.

00:52:11.430 --> 00:52:14.550
And I think it's really
having a positive impact

00:52:14.550 --> 00:52:16.590
on the show,

00:52:16.590 --> 00:52:20.520
because it gives us this an increasingly

00:52:20.520 --> 00:52:25.380
more authentic performance
that's less and less

00:52:25.380 --> 00:52:27.990
of a performance and more of
just an authentic experience

00:52:27.990 --> 00:52:30.330
of us behind that desk.

00:52:30.330 --> 00:52:34.470
So I can't find an
episode where it was like

00:52:34.470 --> 00:52:37.230
I was so excited and then it just bombed

00:52:37.230 --> 00:52:38.190
or didn't come together,

00:52:38.190 --> 00:52:43.190
because they have all the right places

00:52:45.420 --> 00:52:47.603
that matter most there

00:52:47.603 --> 00:52:52.603
and so we can kinda mess around with it.

00:52:52.740 --> 00:52:54.360
And I actually think that,

00:52:54.360 --> 00:52:57.960
so the cliche answer I always give is who?

00:52:57.960 --> 00:52:58.793
Mike.

00:52:59.940 --> 00:53:01.477
But people ask the inverse like,

00:53:01.477 --> 00:53:02.880
"What's your favorite episode?"

00:53:02.880 --> 00:53:06.660
And I'm always like, "The
next one," because we do,

00:53:06.660 --> 00:53:10.770
but what it's just like
we are excited about

00:53:10.770 --> 00:53:12.600
where the show's going.

00:53:12.600 --> 00:53:15.363
We're excited about the
things that we can do.

00:53:16.320 --> 00:53:19.350
That desk, that set, that environment

00:53:19.350 --> 00:53:21.690
is a playground for us.

00:53:21.690 --> 00:53:24.060
And that's another part of
what we've been talking about

00:53:24.060 --> 00:53:28.110
in realizing, hey, what
we've built together,

00:53:28.110 --> 00:53:32.220
how can we take more
advantage of it creatively

00:53:32.220 --> 00:53:35.040
to continue to push boundaries?

00:53:35.040 --> 00:53:37.980
I mean, continue to eat stuff, yes.

00:53:37.980 --> 00:53:40.320
We're gonna keep eating stuff
on Good Mythical Morning

00:53:40.320 --> 00:53:42.693
and we're gonna keep
doing other things too,

00:53:45.437 --> 00:53:47.190
'cause it needs to be fun for us,

00:53:47.190 --> 00:53:50.700
it needs to be fun for the
team that's making it with us

00:53:50.700 --> 00:53:53.820
and blind-siding us
with it a lot of times.

00:53:53.820 --> 00:53:58.140
Yeah, and I think that some of our,

00:53:58.140 --> 00:53:59.400
this wouldn't be a least favorite,

00:53:59.400 --> 00:54:02.280
it's like when am I the most,

00:54:02.280 --> 00:54:05.730
I'm actually changing the
way I think about this now.

00:54:05.730 --> 00:54:07.500
We've talked about this
before on "Ear Biscuits",

00:54:07.500 --> 00:54:10.770
when we try something that we
think is creatively ambitious

00:54:10.770 --> 00:54:13.860
or we do something that isn't
us just eating something

00:54:13.860 --> 00:54:15.960
and then it performs
at like half the views,

00:54:15.960 --> 00:54:17.430
because that's the way the internet works.

00:54:17.430 --> 00:54:20.283
But I know that's not how
you guys feel about it,

00:54:21.120 --> 00:54:25.890
because you're very vocal
when you do like watching us

00:54:25.890 --> 00:54:28.380
take a creative risk and
do something different.

00:54:28.380 --> 00:54:29.880
And not only because you watch,

00:54:29.880 --> 00:54:32.550
many of you watch every
episode or most episodes

00:54:32.550 --> 00:54:33.907
and therefore you're not just like,

00:54:33.907 --> 00:54:35.340
"I'm just coming in for this one

00:54:35.340 --> 00:54:37.680
and I'm coming in for this one."

00:54:37.680 --> 00:54:41.220
So we actually have you in mind more often

00:54:41.220 --> 00:54:43.230
than it may seem like sometimes.

00:54:43.230 --> 00:54:44.940
And again, that's that sort of

00:54:44.940 --> 00:54:47.010
well this has gotta be a
business that supports the team,

00:54:47.010 --> 00:54:51.057
but this is also a show
that is for us and for you

00:54:51.057 --> 00:54:53.490
and for our collective experience.

00:54:53.490 --> 00:54:55.050
So we're gonna keep trying those things

00:54:55.050 --> 00:54:58.830
and we're not going to be
surprised or disappointed

00:54:58.830 --> 00:55:01.830
if some of those things
that may require more effort

00:55:01.830 --> 00:55:04.270
or more creativity or
more time from the team

00:55:05.430 --> 00:55:07.680
don't get as much as many views

00:55:07.680 --> 00:55:09.480
as just sitting down
and trying every flavor

00:55:09.480 --> 00:55:10.383
of a Hot Pocket.

00:55:11.880 --> 00:55:14.730
But we'll continue to try
to make trying every flavor

00:55:14.730 --> 00:55:18.090
of a Hot Pocket as
entertaining and informative,

00:55:18.090 --> 00:55:19.740
because these things matter.

00:55:19.740 --> 00:55:20.940
Right.
Right?

00:55:20.940 --> 00:55:21.773
Right.

00:55:24.330 --> 00:55:27.963
Today we are eating another
block of Velveeta cheese.

00:55:29.340 --> 00:55:31.140
Let's just do that for a month.

