00:55:55.816 --> 00:55:58.020
Are you a horse guy?

00:55:58.020 --> 00:55:59.940
I'm not.
Okay.

00:55:59.940 --> 00:56:02.460
I don't know if that makes
me feel better or worse

00:56:02.460 --> 00:56:03.500
that he's not a horse guy.

00:56:03.500 --> 00:56:04.840
He just bored with it.

00:56:04.840 --> 00:56:09.033
There was a whole level of
humor that you just didn't get.

00:56:09.930 --> 00:56:12.150
Yeah, horse humor.

00:56:12.150 --> 00:56:13.653
Horse humor.
Equine.

00:56:14.730 --> 00:56:19.233
Yeah, yeah, some of them
aren't for everybody.

00:56:20.640 --> 00:56:22.789
It didn't turn out how I
thought it would turn out.

00:56:22.789 --> 00:56:27.789
Yeah, you saw my anxiety on full display

00:56:29.190 --> 00:56:30.990
in that video, boy.

00:56:30.990 --> 00:56:33.990
See it's important for other reasons.

00:56:33.990 --> 00:56:37.560
It's important to be bored
sometimes by our show.

00:56:37.560 --> 00:56:40.020
I had to take down all
my posters of horses

00:56:40.020 --> 00:56:42.150
that I have up around my house.

00:56:42.150 --> 00:56:43.117
Jesse was like,

00:56:43.117 --> 00:56:46.230
"Why are all the horses
in our decor coming down?"

00:56:46.230 --> 00:56:48.840
She loves horse decor.

00:56:48.840 --> 00:56:50.939
It's kind of her specialty.

00:56:50.939 --> 00:56:54.060
We're working on a Thanksgiving episode

00:56:54.060 --> 00:56:55.803
that you were going to hate.

00:56:57.930 --> 00:56:58.763
Awesome.

00:56:59.940 --> 00:57:04.173
It doesn't involve horses
but you will be bored.

00:57:05.880 --> 00:57:07.113
So don't miss it.

00:57:08.002 --> 00:57:09.390
I won't.

00:57:09.390 --> 00:57:14.390
Okay, the last question I'm not 100% sure

00:57:15.210 --> 00:57:16.713
is Abigail Naylor here?

00:57:18.360 --> 00:57:20.970
Oh, we're gonna have to
toss that microphone.

00:57:20.970 --> 00:57:22.170
You're way back there.

00:57:24.330 --> 00:57:25.473
Way back there.

00:57:27.935 --> 00:57:29.761
We just got the yellow card.

00:57:29.761 --> 00:57:30.600
Got the yellow card?

00:57:30.600 --> 00:57:31.433
Yeah.

00:57:32.940 --> 00:57:34.800
This better be a good one,
if this is the last one.

00:57:34.800 --> 00:57:37.920
It is, so okay.

00:57:37.920 --> 00:57:39.330
They're right there.
Sorry I'm late.

00:57:39.330 --> 00:57:40.410
Hello Abigail.

00:57:40.410 --> 00:57:41.580
Hi.

00:57:41.580 --> 00:57:43.117
So you had a question about friendship.

00:57:43.117 --> 00:57:45.900
"What advice would you
give to us for maintaining

00:57:45.900 --> 00:57:47.910
such a meaningful
relationship with each other

00:57:47.910 --> 00:57:49.740
for the many years to come?"

00:57:49.740 --> 00:57:51.180
Yes, that is my question.

00:57:51.180 --> 00:57:52.710
This is my lifelong best friend.

00:57:52.710 --> 00:57:54.747
Well, like 11 years
since we've been friends.

00:57:54.747 --> 00:57:55.770
No, that's nothing.

00:57:55.770 --> 00:57:57.330
So, no, yeah.

00:57:57.330 --> 00:57:58.710
We got a long way to go to compete,

00:57:58.710 --> 00:58:02.400
but she's just like everything to me.

00:58:02.400 --> 00:58:04.320
I moved to a new state
from North Carolina,

00:58:04.320 --> 00:58:06.000
moved to Louisiana with her.

00:58:06.000 --> 00:58:09.330
I knew no one as a
little 11-year-old girl.

00:58:09.330 --> 00:58:10.920
And she was the first person I met

00:58:10.920 --> 00:58:13.110
the very first week I moved to Louisiana.

00:58:13.110 --> 00:58:14.540
And she is just like,

00:58:14.540 --> 00:58:16.500
if you know me, you know her,
if you know her, you know me.

00:58:16.500 --> 00:58:18.480
So I just wanted to get y'all's advice

00:58:18.480 --> 00:58:20.130
'cause y'all have such a great

00:58:20.130 --> 00:58:22.080
lifelong relationship, friendship.

00:58:22.080 --> 00:58:24.630
So yeah, that's just my Clarice.

00:58:24.630 --> 00:58:25.463
So, yeah.

00:58:28.170 --> 00:58:30.598
And what's your friend's name?

00:58:30.598 --> 00:58:32.583
My name is Clarice.

00:58:33.789 --> 00:58:35.460
Miss Clarice?

00:58:35.460 --> 00:58:37.200
Yeah, like "Silence of the Lambs".

00:58:37.200 --> 00:58:39.414
Oh yeah, you haven't, he hasn't seen it.

00:58:39.414 --> 00:58:41.760
We actually both have the first same name.

00:58:41.760 --> 00:58:45.300
We're both Abigail, but she
goes by Clarice, so yeah.

00:58:45.300 --> 00:58:46.230
It makes it simpler.

00:58:46.230 --> 00:58:48.480
Okay, I'm glad you knew her name.

00:58:48.480 --> 00:58:50.443
Yes, I learned that a couple days ago.

00:58:50.443 --> 00:58:51.673
'Cause I was gonna say

00:58:51.673 --> 00:58:55.490
that's the good first step to friendship.

00:58:55.490 --> 00:58:56.400
It is.

00:58:56.400 --> 00:58:58.890
Having the same name also helps.

00:58:58.890 --> 00:59:00.030
That too, she didn't tell me that

00:59:00.030 --> 00:59:01.620
until two weeks after we knew each other.

00:59:01.620 --> 00:59:03.600
She's like, "Oh, my name's
Abigail by the way."

00:59:03.600 --> 00:59:06.477
I was like, "That's good
information to have Clarice."

00:59:07.620 --> 00:59:09.393
Two weeks to find out her name.

00:59:10.320 --> 00:59:13.950
All right, we gotta give
some good advice here.

00:59:13.950 --> 00:59:15.513
It's the last question.

00:59:18.060 --> 00:59:19.320
Well, we've answered this,

00:59:19.320 --> 00:59:20.790
we have answered this question,

00:59:20.790 --> 00:59:23.973
but we wanted to talk about it again.

00:59:25.461 --> 00:59:28.800
Friendship, is not easy.

00:59:28.800 --> 00:59:29.633
No.

00:59:31.244 --> 00:59:34.740
I'll sing a lot guys, 'cause I'm a guy

00:59:34.740 --> 00:59:38.250
and I talk to guys about
friendships sometimes

00:59:38.250 --> 00:59:41.057
and they're like,

00:59:41.057 --> 00:59:44.190
"Well, it's tough for guys sometimes."

00:59:44.190 --> 00:59:47.670
We're guys, we have an
extraordinary friendship.

00:59:47.670 --> 00:59:51.660
There's a lot wrapped up into it,

00:59:51.660 --> 00:59:54.660
so there's a lot at play here.

00:59:54.660 --> 00:59:59.660
In some ways, friendship
or even best friendship

00:59:59.700 --> 01:00:02.850
is an inadequate description of the nature

01:00:02.850 --> 01:00:05.460
of our relationship.

01:00:05.460 --> 01:00:09.390
I mean, so we are best friends,

01:00:09.390 --> 01:00:13.020
but when you're that and
you're also business partners

01:00:13.020 --> 01:00:17.400
and your performers

01:00:17.400 --> 01:00:22.400
who a cornerstone of your
performance is friendship.

01:00:24.660 --> 01:00:26.703
I mean there could be some,

01:00:28.080 --> 01:00:30.780
there's some unique challenges for us,

01:00:30.780 --> 01:00:34.893
because there's a lot wrapped up in it.

01:00:36.221 --> 01:00:39.810
It's certainly very important to us

01:00:39.810 --> 01:00:42.030
to actually be friends,

01:00:42.030 --> 01:00:46.050
because we care about each other.

01:00:46.050 --> 01:00:48.870
But also, and then secondarily,

01:00:48.870 --> 01:00:53.870
because this doesn't work if
we're not actually friends,

01:00:53.940 --> 01:00:56.151
that would really suck.

01:00:56.151 --> 01:00:57.480
You're saying that

01:00:57.480 --> 01:00:59.340
if you make your relationship the product

01:00:59.340 --> 01:01:01.040
and then the relationship is over,

01:01:02.220 --> 01:01:03.960
that is a big problem.

01:01:03.960 --> 01:01:05.760
We've never seen that happen before.

01:01:08.730 --> 01:01:12.651
It's interesting, somebody
sent me something recently

01:01:12.651 --> 01:01:16.440
that talked about how
the number of friends

01:01:16.440 --> 01:01:19.770
in people's lives decreased dramatically,

01:01:19.770 --> 01:01:23.140
because of the pandemic and specifically

01:01:24.420 --> 01:01:25.830
number of close friends.

01:01:25.830 --> 01:01:27.630
I thought you were gonna
say because of children,

01:01:27.630 --> 01:01:32.400
which is also children
will kill friendships.

01:01:32.400 --> 01:01:33.233
No, but specifically-
Especially babies.

01:01:33.233 --> 01:01:35.430
'Cause of the social dynamics-

01:01:35.430 --> 01:01:36.263
Oh my gosh.

01:01:36.263 --> 01:01:37.500
The social dynamics-
I'll remember that.

01:01:37.500 --> 01:01:40.383
The way that things
changed with the pandemic.

01:01:41.730 --> 01:01:45.750
There is I would say an
alarming percentage of people

01:01:45.750 --> 01:01:50.310
who do not have one close
friend, that has increased.

01:01:50.310 --> 01:01:52.137
But the number of, and
this is something that

01:01:52.137 --> 01:01:54.660
has just been moving in
that direction culturally

01:01:54.660 --> 01:01:55.710
for a really long time.

01:01:55.710 --> 01:01:58.320
So I do think it's more
important than ever.

01:01:58.320 --> 01:02:00.420
One of the ways that we
have answered this question

01:02:00.420 --> 01:02:03.810
in the past, we always
talk about communication.

01:02:03.810 --> 01:02:05.460
I wanna come back to communication,

01:02:05.460 --> 01:02:07.260
because one of the things that I've said,

01:02:07.260 --> 01:02:10.203
and then we've talked about
this recently on our own,

01:02:11.160 --> 01:02:14.070
is the way we would answer this question

01:02:14.070 --> 01:02:18.180
on one of the tours,

01:02:18.180 --> 01:02:20.610
I think it was maybe
Bleak Creek or whatever,

01:02:20.610 --> 01:02:21.930
'cause there was a
question about friendship

01:02:21.930 --> 01:02:24.870
that would come up a lot
and we would talk about

01:02:24.870 --> 01:02:29.220
how having something
that you're a common goal

01:02:29.220 --> 01:02:32.610
was key to a long
friendship and the fact that

01:02:32.610 --> 01:02:35.460
we were building something together

01:02:35.460 --> 01:02:38.190
and so you've got this baby
that you shared together

01:02:38.190 --> 01:02:41.710
in the same way that like
a marriage may have kids

01:02:43.650 --> 01:02:45.930
you pointed out, which I agree with

01:02:45.930 --> 01:02:48.660
and why I think that that is somewhat true

01:02:48.660 --> 01:02:49.800
and I do think it's actually true,

01:02:49.800 --> 01:02:51.690
because for a lot of the
reasons that we're saying,

01:02:51.690 --> 01:02:53.100
there's a lot of things at stake

01:02:53.100 --> 01:02:54.930
and there's also a lot of
things that bring us back

01:02:54.930 --> 01:02:59.930
into each other's lives 'cause
both of us tend to kind of,

01:03:00.540 --> 01:03:02.310
I mean, you're more extroverted than me,

01:03:02.310 --> 01:03:05.820
but in a lot of ways we can go
a pretty long period of time

01:03:05.820 --> 01:03:08.490
without having meaningful
interactions with somebody,

01:03:08.490 --> 01:03:10.710
'cause we have so many things going on

01:03:10.710 --> 01:03:12.300
and there's a lot of
people maybe back home

01:03:12.300 --> 01:03:14.970
in North Carolina that
were good friends with us,

01:03:14.970 --> 01:03:17.040
that we're just not the greatest at

01:03:17.040 --> 01:03:18.700
staying in touch with people

01:03:19.650 --> 01:03:21.180
are initiating and maintaining

01:03:21.180 --> 01:03:22.950
those long distance friendships.

01:03:22.950 --> 01:03:26.010
So one of the things we're
thrown into each other's worlds

01:03:26.010 --> 01:03:27.150
over and over again all the time.

01:03:27.150 --> 01:03:28.440
We're constantly in each other's worlds.

01:03:28.440 --> 01:03:30.200
So that's one of the things.

01:03:30.200 --> 01:03:31.830
So there's no outta sight outta mind,

01:03:31.830 --> 01:03:34.080
that's never gonna happen with us.

01:03:34.080 --> 01:03:38.590
But you kind of pointed out
the inadequacy in that analogy,

01:03:40.410 --> 01:03:42.870
because the negative way of seeing that

01:03:42.870 --> 01:03:45.990
is you would say that
if it were a marriage

01:03:45.990 --> 01:03:49.860
and people were just in the
marriage, because of the kids,

01:03:49.860 --> 01:03:54.210
then the day that the kids
leave, the marriage is over.

01:03:54.210 --> 01:03:56.733
And that happens all too often.

01:03:57.720 --> 01:03:59.250
And you made the point that,

01:03:59.250 --> 01:04:02.610
well that's only part of the answer

01:04:02.610 --> 01:04:04.800
and probably not even the most
important part of the answer.

01:04:04.800 --> 01:04:07.650
The answer is the friendship is valuable,

01:04:07.650 --> 01:04:10.170
because the friendship is valuable

01:04:10.170 --> 01:04:14.190
and the friendship
remains and is important,

01:04:14.190 --> 01:04:17.070
because of the value that
the friendship brings,

01:04:17.070 --> 01:04:20.787
not because of the thing that
you guys are building together

01:04:20.787 --> 01:04:23.550
and the way it benefits
from your friendship.

01:04:23.550 --> 01:04:25.380
If that makes sense.

01:04:25.380 --> 01:04:29.370
Yeah, I think definitely.

01:04:29.370 --> 01:04:33.540
When we're both excited

01:04:33.540 --> 01:04:35.670
and going after the same thing

01:04:35.670 --> 01:04:37.560
and we're in this

01:04:37.560 --> 01:04:39.690
especially if we're in
like a creative flow,

01:04:39.690 --> 01:04:42.240
it's very exhilarating to be there

01:04:42.240 --> 01:04:45.210
with your lifelong best friend and to say,

01:04:45.210 --> 01:04:47.880
we're going after this thing,

01:04:47.880 --> 01:04:49.450
we're making this thing

01:04:51.291 --> 01:04:55.290
and it's kind of a magical synergy.

01:04:55.290 --> 01:04:58.830
It does feel great and it does edify me

01:04:58.830 --> 01:05:01.650
and I think our connection,

01:05:01.650 --> 01:05:06.540
but there's also that personal side to it

01:05:06.540 --> 01:05:10.290
that's like, yeah, but
we're also actually friends.

01:05:10.290 --> 01:05:14.250
I'm restating what you're
saying in other words that,

01:05:14.250 --> 01:05:16.650
and I think this is kind
of where our answer is,

01:05:16.650 --> 01:05:20.460
is that it takes a lot of work

01:05:20.460 --> 01:05:24.903
to maintain a relationship,

01:05:25.920 --> 01:05:29.310
and for it, I think it's very important

01:05:29.310 --> 01:05:34.310
that we have given ourselves
permission to say this is,

01:05:36.960 --> 01:05:40.860
we're committed to this
and it requires work

01:05:40.860 --> 01:05:45.860
and there's times when it's not
going as well as other times

01:05:46.410 --> 01:05:50.010
and given ourselves and
each other permission

01:05:50.010 --> 01:05:55.010
to express our needs in the friendship

01:05:56.700 --> 01:05:58.360
is something that

01:06:00.120 --> 01:06:01.860
I think we're still learning.

01:06:01.860 --> 01:06:04.110
I mean, we're still growing as individuals

01:06:04.110 --> 01:06:06.270
like we talked about earlier,

01:06:06.270 --> 01:06:08.100
getting in touch with your emotions,

01:06:08.100 --> 01:06:11.580
understanding that there's a
lot more of those out there

01:06:11.580 --> 01:06:14.553
that can be a part of
your experience like that.

01:06:15.870 --> 01:06:18.300
It's a similar experience

01:06:18.300 --> 01:06:22.470
of knowing how to apply

01:06:22.470 --> 01:06:26.010
the principles of
relationship to a friendship,

01:06:26.010 --> 01:06:28.770
'cause you think, well
you can fall in this trap

01:06:28.770 --> 01:06:33.630
that friends should just be
friends, because it's easy.

01:06:33.630 --> 01:06:36.390
You don't make friends,

01:06:36.390 --> 01:06:37.950
your closest friends are people

01:06:37.950 --> 01:06:41.940
that you just find
yourselves hanging out with

01:06:41.940 --> 01:06:42.877
and having a good time with,

01:06:42.877 --> 01:06:45.150
"Oh, I find myself liking this person,"

01:06:45.150 --> 01:06:50.150
but that's not, a friendship
is not gonna thrive longterm

01:06:51.690 --> 01:06:55.057
if you don't give yourself
permission to say,

01:06:55.057 --> 01:06:58.620
"No, this is a relationship that needs,

01:06:58.620 --> 01:07:01.740
that requires work and energy."

01:07:01.740 --> 01:07:06.740
And a big part of that is
being vulnerable to say,

01:07:07.327 --> 01:07:12.327
"Hey, in this particular
time I have a need

01:07:12.330 --> 01:07:17.330
that's not being met that in
our friendship," or vice versa.

01:07:17.610 --> 01:07:20.130
And so, and we've had those conversations

01:07:20.130 --> 01:07:21.720
and I think that's something

01:07:21.720 --> 01:07:24.060
that we haven't shared publicly.

01:07:24.060 --> 01:07:26.910
We just say we leave it at communication.

01:07:26.910 --> 01:07:29.640
But I think that's a little
more specific version

01:07:29.640 --> 01:07:32.463
of that same thing,
'cause it's easy to say,

01:07:32.463 --> 01:07:34.200
well you just gotta communicate,

01:07:34.200 --> 01:07:36.090
you gotta talk things out.

01:07:36.090 --> 01:07:39.450
But even after all these years

01:07:39.450 --> 01:07:42.810
when you find that if
you have a certain need

01:07:42.810 --> 01:07:44.550
in the friendship that's not being met,

01:07:44.550 --> 01:07:46.416
it's a hard thing to say,

01:07:46.416 --> 01:07:49.560
it's a very vulnerable moment.

01:07:49.560 --> 01:07:53.110
And then on the other
side of it though is this

01:07:56.700 --> 01:07:59.820
a renewed connection and a renewed bond

01:07:59.820 --> 01:08:02.730
that I think we've been
through that a number of times.

01:08:02.730 --> 01:08:06.450
Yeah so, this may seem like a tautology

01:08:06.450 --> 01:08:08.700
or circular reasoning or
whatever you wanna call it,

01:08:08.700 --> 01:08:13.020
but the best way to maintain
a friendship longterm

01:08:13.020 --> 01:08:14.853
is to continue to be friends.

01:08:16.680 --> 01:08:19.150
And don't expect it to be easy.

01:08:19.150 --> 01:08:21.720
And understanding that it's a commitment.

01:08:21.720 --> 01:08:24.330
And I think the same thing
applies to relationships.

01:08:24.330 --> 01:08:27.693
We could really quickly transfer
this into marriage advice.

01:08:28.527 --> 01:08:32.460
When we got into our
marriages it was under

01:08:32.460 --> 01:08:34.980
very specific understanding

01:08:34.980 --> 01:08:36.630
and we were in a very specific place

01:08:36.630 --> 01:08:38.460
in terms of our worldview.

01:08:38.460 --> 01:08:41.793
And in that worldview divorce
was not really an option.

01:08:43.040 --> 01:08:47.220
Now we are different people at this point

01:08:47.220 --> 01:08:48.870
and I think that there
are legitimate reasons

01:08:48.870 --> 01:08:52.080
for relationships to end
and marriages to end,

01:08:52.080 --> 01:08:57.080
but I think that we still
have to approach our marriages

01:08:57.464 --> 01:08:59.943
as if divorce is not an option.

01:09:01.525 --> 01:09:04.170
Obviously, there are circumstances
that could lead to that,

01:09:04.170 --> 01:09:06.750
but I think that you have to go in

01:09:06.750 --> 01:09:07.920
with the level of commitment

01:09:07.920 --> 01:09:10.890
that this isn't going to be based on

01:09:10.890 --> 01:09:13.073
continual feelings of romance

01:09:13.073 --> 01:09:15.060
and friendships are not gonna be based on

01:09:15.060 --> 01:09:17.580
continual feelings of compatibility

01:09:17.580 --> 01:09:20.073
or this, the vibes are always good.

01:09:20.970 --> 01:09:22.556
If that's what it's based on,

01:09:22.556 --> 01:09:24.990
the moment that that ends,
it's just like, all right,

01:09:24.990 --> 01:09:27.690
I got more convenient people or whatever,

01:09:27.690 --> 01:09:29.250
it's something that's easier for me.

01:09:29.250 --> 01:09:31.290
So I think that that's where
the communication comes in.

01:09:31.290 --> 01:09:33.390
The communication is because things are,

01:09:33.390 --> 01:09:36.120
when you're work on so
many things together

01:09:36.120 --> 01:09:37.320
and you're together all the time

01:09:37.320 --> 01:09:39.420
in the same way that you
get annoyed at your brother

01:09:39.420 --> 01:09:40.593
or your your sibling,

01:09:40.593 --> 01:09:43.143
that's the kind of
relationship that we have.

01:09:43.980 --> 01:09:47.460
And so, understanding when
that's beginning to happen

01:09:47.460 --> 01:09:50.880
and knowing when
conversations are required

01:09:50.880 --> 01:09:52.950
in order to kind of break
through that and move

01:09:52.950 --> 01:09:55.500
to the to the next step
has been really pivotal.

01:09:55.500 --> 01:09:56.490
Is that good?

01:09:56.490 --> 01:09:57.390
That's perfect.

01:09:57.390 --> 01:09:59.040
It's more than enough, thank you.

